@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Hurt boxes") {
        In the previous lesson, we made the player able to squash monsters
        by landing on them. Now let's add the consequence: when the player
        runs into a snake from the side, the player dies.

        We'll add a second
        [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_area3d.html#class-area3d)
        to the mob - we will call it a **hurt box**: a capsule along the snake's body that
        detects the player running into it. The trick is that the hurt box
        only kills the player if they're on the floor, so jumping *over* a
        snake is a safe strategy.

        When the player dies, they emit a `hit` signal, and `Main` stops
        spawning new mobs.

        @Image(source: 08-03.capsule-shape-settings.png)
    }
    @Section(title: "Adding the hurt box") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The hurt box is the area that damages the player. We want it to
            cover the snake's body, so a capsule oriented along the body
            works well. We'll place this one under the `Pivot` so it rotates
            and moves with the model.

        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Go back to the 3D view.
                
                Open the `Mob` scene. Select the `Pivot` node and add an
                [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_area3d.html#class-area3d)
                child named `HurtBox`. Add a
                [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_collisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
                child to it.

                @Image(source: 08-02.add-hurt-box-area.png, alt: "Add an Area3D named HurtBox under Pivot with a CollisionShape3D child")
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, assign a
                [CapsuleShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_capsuleshape3d.html#class-capsuleshape3d)
                to the _Shape_ property. Tap it to open the new resource and set its _Radius_ to about `0.6` and
                _Height_ to about `2.5`. 
                
                In the Transform section: Rotate the capsule `90` degrees on
                `Y` and `Z` so it lies along the snake's body, and position it so
                it covers the head of the snake. For mine, `Z` is set to `-1.2` in the position parameter

                @Image(source: 08-03.capsule-shape-settings.png, alt: "Resize and orient the capsule along the snake's body")
            }
            @Step {
                Select the `HurtBox` node and, in the _Signals_ tab, connect
                its `body_entered` signal to the `Mob` node.

                @Image(source: 08-04.connect-hurt-box-signal.png, alt: "Connect the HurtBox body_entered signal to the Mob")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Coding the hurt box's callback") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The callback we just connected will call `body.die()` when the
            player touches the snake's body. We only kill the player if
            they're on the floor — that's what makes jumping over a snake
            safe. The player doesn't have a `die()` function yet; we'll add
            it next.

        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the `mob.gd` script if it's not open. 
                Next, add the following to the `_on_hurt_box_body_entered`
                callback. It bails out early if the entering body has no
                `die` method, and again if the body is in the air — so a
                player jumping over the snake is untouched.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-06.gd, previousFile: mob-3d-05.gd)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Coding the player's death") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The mob's callback calls `body.die()`, so the player needs a
            `die()` function. When it runs, the player emits a `hit` signal
            — `Main` will listen for that to stop the game — and then frees
            itself.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the `player.gd` script. We add the `hit` signal at the top and
                the `die()` function near the bottom.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-10.gd, previousFile: player-3d-09.gd)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Ending the game") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            When the player dies, the `Player` emits its `hit` signal. We
            can use that to stop spawning new mobs. Connect the signal to
            the `Main` node and stop the `MobTimer` in reaction.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Switch back to 3D mode and open `main.tscn`. Select the `Player` node, and in the
                _Signals_ tab, connect its `hit` signal to the `Main` node. Name the function `_on_player_hit`.

                @Image(source: 08-08.player-hit-signal-connection.png)
            }
            @Step {
                In the `_on_player_hit()` function that was just added, stop
                the `MobTimer`.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-04.gd, previousFile: main-3d-03.gd)
            }
            If you try the game now, running into a snake on the ground
            kills the player and the monsters stop spawning, while the
            remaining ones leave the screen. Jump over a snake and you're
            safe.

            > Note: If you had tried the game earlier before we did this, 
            > you'll notice that the game no longer crashes or displays an error when
            > the player dies. Because we stop the `MobTimer`, it no longer
            > triggers the `_on_mob_timer_timeout()` function — and the
            > player node has been `queue_free`'d, so any subsequent
            > reference to `$Player` in the timer callback would fail.

            You can pat yourself on the back: you prototyped a complete 3D
            game, even if it's still a bit rough. From there, we'll add a
            score, the option to retry the game, and you'll see how you can
            make the game feel much more alive with the model's built-in
            animations.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Code checkpoint") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Here are the complete scripts for the `Mob`, `Player`, and
            `Main` nodes at this point, for reference. You can use them to
            compare and check your code.

            @Image(source: 08-09.code-checkpoint-reference.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Starting with `mob.gd`.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-06.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Next is `player.gd`.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-10.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, the `main.gd` script.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-04.gd, reset: true)
            }
            In the next lesson, we'll add the score and the option to
            retry the game.
        }
    }
}
